/*****************************************************************************
    jitSlotT2 -- OP TWO OF THE SLOT MIGRATION, the jitCmp specimen. 2026-08-17.

    Certifies that the greater-than operator emits through its gJitEmitter slot.
    Sibling of jitSlotT, which certifies the multiply. Read that one first; it
    carries the argument for why the slot COUNT and not the answer is the row
    that matters, and the measured negative control behind it.

    WHY A COMPARISON AND NOT A SECOND ARITHMETIC. The selector population the
    slot model retires has THREE families -- jitOp, jitCmp, jitUnary -- and the
    pathfinder proved the slot on jitOp only. A binary-shaped assumption in the
    slot signature would hide behind a second jitOp member and surface many ops
    into the sweep instead of at op two, which is where it is cheapest to find.

    ⚠ THE ANSWER IS THAT THERE WAS NO SUCH ASSUMPTION, recorded because a null
    result still cost something to obtain. jitEmitCompare has the same shape as
    jitEmitBinary -- argument and target in, target back -- even though its
    RESULT type differs, an i1 rather than an operand-typed value. So the
    two-argument slot spans jitOp and jitCmp with nothing special-cased and this
    shim was the same three lines as the first. That is the finding op two was
    sent to get: the pathfinder generalizes.

    WHY A SEPARATE FILE AND NOT A SECOND SECTION IN jitSlotT. A rung gets ONE
    testing call and then jitRefire forever; a second testing call in the same
    process re-enters the sequential-state corruption the refire scaffold exists
    to make unconstructable. Two ops therefore means two files -- and that also
    buys per-op diagnosis, since a joint rung asserting a count of two could not
    say WHICH op had stopped using its slot.

    THE COUNT IS PER PROCESS, which is why this rung wants one and not two. The
    combined observation lives in incant jitJR, which contains both a multiply
    and a greater-than: it read 1 before op two landed and 2 after, on identical
    products. That is the 1 to 2 movement this campaign tracks.

    ⚠ FIRE TWO INVERTS THE OPERANDS so the answers differ. One of the two is a
    zero, so the pair is chosen such that the other is not -- a row that a
    do-nothing default could also satisfy asserts nothing on its own.

    ⚠ THE OUTPUT FIELD IS INITIALIZED BEFORE THE FIRE. An uninitialized field
    returns its own tag when read (bear-trap 26); initialized, the compiled
    store lands and the read-back is real. Measured both ways in jitSlotT.

    ⚠ THE JITTED HALF IS FIRST, bear-trap 25.
*****************************************************************************/

register(JitLadder);
define
    jsgA;
    jsgB;
    jsgOut;
    jsgGT code={
        jsgOut = jsgA > jsgB;
        };
    ;

jsgA = 6; jsgB = 3; jsgOut = 0;
print "JSG fire 1 setup : jsgA =" jsgA " jsgB =" jsgB:;
testing(jsgGT);
print "JSG fire 1 result: jsgOut =" jsgOut " (want 1)":;

jsgA = 3; jsgB = 6; jsgOut = 0;
print "JSG fire 2 setup : jsgA =" jsgA " jsgB =" jsgB " (recompile: NONE)":;
jitRefire();
print "JSG fire 2 result: jsgOut =" jsgOut " (want 0)":;

jsgA = 6; jsgB = 3; jsgOut = 0;
jsgGT();
print "JSG interpreted  : jsgOut =" jsgOut " (the oracle -- want 1)":;
print "JITSLOT2 SENTINEL -- run reached the end":;
stop();
